# Line endings, decided once here rather than inferred per file.
#
# Git treats a file as binary when it cannot be sure it is text, and a file it has
# decided is binary is stored EXACTLY as it is on disk — no normalisation. On a
# Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` every other file is stored LF and
# converted both ways; a "binary" source file keeps its CRLF inside the repository.
#
# That is not cosmetic, and it cost a real defect. `src/entrypoints/tools/mcp/shell.ts`
# was recorded that way. A tool that rewrote the file rather than appending to it
# produced a diff of the entire file — every line added and every line removed —
# and a real defect travelled inside that noise unremarked: a sentence in the shell
# tool's own description had lost the word that made it mean anything, and no
# reviewer could see it. A 3-line change arrived as 192 lines.
#
# `text=auto` is the rule that already applies to every other file here, written
# down so it applies to all of them: decide from the content, and normalise.
* text=auto

# Not text, and never normalised. A PNG is a test fixture whose bytes ARE the test
# input, so a rewritten file is a different fixture rather than the same one stored
# differently.
*.png -text

# Text, said outright. `text=auto` ASKS, and its answer for this file is wrong: the
# file has no NUL byte and is valid UTF-8, yet git recorded it as binary and
# `text=auto` honours that. Without this line the rule above changes nothing here.
#
# A pattern rather than the single path, because the detection that went wrong is
# about content and would go wrong for the next such file too. Nothing else under
# `tools/mcp/` is anything but UTF-8 source.
src/entrypoints/tools/mcp/*.ts text eol=lf
